Result 
Quiz
A two-way repeated-measures ANOVA was conducted to examine the effects of Technique (AdaptiveFlow, PeriphGuide, ScaleStable, AdaptiveLock) and Environment (Open, Room transition) on quiz scores (out of 12), a measure of content comprehension/retention. Shapiro-Wilk tests indicated that several conditions departed from normality (DO, DR, OO, WO, WR; all p < .05), reflecting a ceiling effect given many participants scored near the maximum under AdaptiveFlow, PeriphGuide, and AdaptiveLock. Results revealed a significant main effect of Technique, F(3, 45) = 21.46, p < .001, partial η² = .589 — an even larger effect than observed for dwell time — but no significant main effect of Environment, F(1, 15) = 2.18, p = .160, partial η² = .127, and no significant Technique × Environment interaction, F(3, 45) = 0.06, p = .979, partial η² = .004. Bonferroni-corrected pairwise comparisons showed that ScaleStable (M = 8.69) produced significantly lower quiz scores than AdaptiveFlow (M = 11.13, p < .001), PeriphGuide (M = 10.19, p = .033), and AdaptiveLock (M = 10.91, p < .001); AdaptiveFlow also scored significantly higher than PeriphGuide (p = .035), while AdaptiveFlow and AdaptiveLock did not differ from each other. Follow-up one-way ANOVAs within each environment confirmed that ScaleStable remained the weakest technique both in the Open condition, F(3, 45) = 10.47, p < .001, partial η² = .411, and during the Room transition, F(3, 45) = 11.32, p < .001, partial η² = .430, and paired-samples t-tests found no significant Open-versus-Room difference in quiz scores for any individual technique (all p > .35). Taken together, these results indicate that ScaleStable consistently impairs content comprehension regardless of environment — mirroring its disadvantage in dwell time — but that, unlike dwell time, comprehension was not measurably affected by the room transition itself, suggesting that the reduced visual attention to content during environment changes did not translate into a corresponding loss of retained information, at least as captured by this quiz.

Temporal Availability 
A two-way repeated-measures ANOVA was conducted to examine the effects of Technique (AdaptiveFlow, PeriphGuide, ScaleStable, AdaptiveLock) and Environment (Open, Room transition) on the Temporal Availability subscale (7-point scale). The main effect of Technique was significant, F(3, 45) = 5.82, p = .002, partial η² = .279, while the main effect of Environment, F(1, 15) = 0.28, p = .606, partial η² = .018, and the Technique × Environment interaction, F(3, 45) = 1.29, p = .289, partial η² = .079, were not. Notably, inspection of the cell means revealed a crossover pattern underlying this non-significant interaction: AdaptiveFlow (6.10 → 5.94), ScaleStable (5.44 → 5.04), and AdaptiveLock (5.81 → 5.58) all declined from Open to Room transition, whereas PeriphGuide was the sole technique to increase (5.65 → 6.10) — a directional reversal (paired t(15) = -1.479, p = .160) that likely attenuated the omnibus interaction test despite a non-trivial effect size, since three techniques pulling one direction and one pulling the opposite direction partially cancel out in an averaged F-test. This crossover was corroborated by environment-specific one-way ANOVAs: the multivariate test of the Technique effect was not significant within the Open condition alone, F(3, 13) = 2.46, p = .109, partial η² = .362 (with AdaptiveFlow numerically higher than ScaleStable, pairwise p = .092), but became significant within the Room-transition condition, F(3, 13) = 7.86, p = .003, partial η² = .645, where ScaleStable (M = 5.04) scored significantly lower than both AdaptiveFlow (M = 5.94, p = .037) and PeriphGuide (M = 6.10, p = .002). Together, these results suggest that Technique differences in perceived temporal availability are largely masked in the Open environment but sharpen considerably during room transitions — driven in part by PeriphGuide's distinctive resilience (and even improvement) under that more demanding condition, in contrast to the consistent decline shown by the other three techniques.


Dwell time on content 

A two-way repeated-measures ANOVA was conducted to examine the effects of Technique (AdaptiveFlow, PeriphGuide, ScaleStable, AdaptiveLock) and Environment (Open, Room transition) on participants' dwell time on content (%); Shapiro-Wilk tests confirmed all eight conditions were normally distributed (all p > .05). Results revealed a significant main effect of Technique, F(3, 45) = 16.20, p < .001, partial η² = .52, and a significant main effect of Environment, F(1, 15) = 14.00, p = .002, partial η² = .48, with no significant Technique × Environment interaction, F(3, 45) = 0.42, p = .741, partial η² = .03. Bonferroni-corrected pairwise comparisons showed that ScaleStable (M = 38.46%) elicited significantly lower content dwell time than AdaptiveFlow (M = 54.19%), PeriphGuide (M = 54.13%), and AdaptiveLock (M = 54.87%, all p ≤ .001), with no significant differences among the other three techniques; participants also spent significantly more time on content in the Open environment (M = 54.84%) than during the Room transition (M = 45.99%), a difference of 8.85 percentage points (p = .002). Follow-up one-way ANOVAs conducted separately within each environment confirmed that ScaleStable remained the weakest-performing technique both in the Open condition, F(3, 45) = 12.75, p < .001, partial η² = .46, and during the Room transition, F(3, 45) = 6.22, p = .001, partial η² = .29 — indicating its disadvantage was robust across environments, though somewhat attenuated during room transitions. Paired-samples t-tests further probed the environment effect within each technique and revealed a more nuanced picture than the omnibus interaction suggested: the Open-to-Room decline in content dwell time was statistically significant for PeriphGuide (Δ = 11.54, t(15) = 2.87, p = .012) and ScaleStable (Δ = 10.15, t(15) = 2.24, p = .041), but only marginal for AdaptiveFlow (Δ = 8.28, t(15) = 1.89, p = .079) and non-significant for AdaptiveLock (Δ = 5.41, t(15) = 1.32, p = .207). Taken together, these findings suggest that ScaleStable consistently underperforms in sustaining attention to meeting content regardless of environment, while AdaptiveLock appears comparatively robust to the disruption introduced by room transitions — a pattern the omnibus interaction test, likely underpowered at n = 16, did not detect but which the environment-specific and technique-specific contrasts make visible.


RTLX

A two-way repeated-measures ANOVA was conducted to examine the effects of Technique (AdaptiveFlow, PeriphGuide, ScaleStable, AdaptiveLock) and Environment (Open, Room transition) on overall subjective workload, as measured by the Raw NASA-TLX (RTLX, 0–100 scale). In contrast to the Temporal Availability subscale, this global workload measure was dominated by Environment rather than Technique: the main effect of Environment was large and highly significant, F(1, 15) = 27.62, p < .001, partial η² = .648, with workload markedly higher during the Room transition (M = 58.23) than in the Open condition (M = 37.78), a difference of 20.44 points. The main effect of Technique was not significant, F(3, 45) = 1.49, p = .229, partial η² = .091, nor was the Technique × Environment interaction, F(3, 45) = 1.35, p = .270, partial η² = .083; no pairwise technique comparison reached significance under the omnibus model. Environment-specific analyses confirmed that Technique differences remained non-significant within the Open condition, F(3, 45) = 1.02, p = .394, partial η² = .063, and were only weakly suggested within the Room-transition condition — non-significant by the univariate sphericity-corrected test, F(3, 45) = 1.95, p = .164 (Mauchly's W indicated a sphericity violation, p = .024), but significant by the multivariate test, F(3, 13) = 4.46, p = .023, partial η² = .507, driven by PeriphGuide (M = 63.65) scoring significantly higher than AdaptiveLock (M = 54.56, p = .012). Most notably, paired-samples t-tests revealed that workload increased significantly from Open to Room transition for every technique — AdaptiveFlow (Δ = 17.79, t(15) = -2.53, p = .023), PeriphGuide (Δ = 27.96, t(15) = -8.43, p < .001), ScaleStable (Δ = 15.77, t(15) = -2.63, p = .019), and AdaptiveLock (Δ = 20.25, t(15) = -3.88, p = .001) — with PeriphGuide showing by far the largest and most reliable increase. This pattern contrasts with the crossover observed for Temporal Availability, where PeriphGuide was the sole technique that improved during the room transition: taken together, the two measures suggest that while PeriphGuide may preserve a subjective sense of having enough time during a room transition, it does not spare participants from a substantially greater overall workload cost, and in fact incurs the largest such cost among the four techniques.

SUS
To assess perceived usability across the four anchoring techniques, System Usability Scale (SUS) scores were computed for each condition (delay, offset, sizesame, walkworld) based on 16 participants. Descriptive statistics revealed a range of usability ratings across conditions, with walkworld (M = 83.75, SD = 11.47) and sizesame (M = 81.88, SD = 14.59) scoring above the accepted average usability benchmark of 68 (Bangor et al., 2008), while offset (M = 71.56, SD = 10.12) and delay (M = 64.38, SD = 11.81) scored near or below this threshold. Shapiro-Wilk tests confirmed that SUS scores were normally distributed for all four conditions (all p > .05).
A one-way repeated-measures ANOVA revealed a significant main effect of technique on SUS scores, F(3, 45) = 6.96, p < .001, partial η² = .32, indicating that perceived usability differed substantially across the four anchoring techniques. Bonferroni-corrected pairwise comparisons showed that delay was rated significantly lower than both sizesame (p = .011) and walkworld (p = .006), and that offset was rated significantly lower than walkworld (p = .016). No significant differences were found between sizesame and walkworld (p = 1.000), nor between delay and offset (p = .996), suggesting that the four techniques clustered into two distinct usability groups: a higher-usability group (sizesame, walkworld) and a lower-usability group (delay, offset).
